; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Silly Sentence Machine
; CATEGORY: Classic Logo
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     words, grammar, recursion, property lists, random
; SUMMARY:  A tiny grammar stored in property lists grows random, grammatical and quite silly sentences.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Prints ten random sentences such as
;     "the purple turtle quietly tickles a sleepy teacher near the moon."
;   Every sentence follows the same rules of grammar, but the words are
;   picked at random, so you get a new batch every run.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Run it again for new sentences. Type  print expand "sentence
;   in the command center for one more.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * a grammar: rules that say how bigger parts are built from smaller ones
;   * property lists (PPROP and GPROP) as a little dictionary
;   * a recursive operation that expands a part into words
;   * PICK to choose at random, MAP.SE to join the pieces into a sentence
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Add your own nouns and verbs, maybe names of your friends.
;   * Add a rule so a sentence can be "sentence and sentence".
;   * Make a question rule: "does the turtle tickle the moon?"
; ============================================================

; The very first Logo, in the late 1960s, had no turtle at all. It
; worked with words and sentences, and making the computer write
; funny sentences was one of the things children did with it. This
; program keeps that spirit, using a grammar the way a linguist would.

; RULE stores the choices for one part of speech. Each choice is a list
; of parts; a part that has no rule of its own is an actual word.
to rule :part :choices
  pprop "grammar :part :choices
end

to grammar
  rule "sentence     [[nounphrase verbphrase .] [nounphrase verbphrase place .]]
  rule "nounphrase   [[article noun] [article adjective noun] [article adjective noun]]
  rule "verbphrase   [[verb nounphrase] [adverb verb nounphrase] [verb nounphrase]]
  rule "place        [[near article noun] [under the bed] [on the moon] [in a teapot]]
  rule "article      [[the] [a] [my] [every]]
  rule "adjective    [[purple] [sleepy] [enormous] [tiny] [grumpy] [polite] [wobbly]]
  rule "noun         [[turtle] [teacher] [banana] [robot] [giraffe] [cloud] [pirate]]
  rule "verb         [[tickles] [paints] [follows] [sings to] [borrows] [hugs] [ignores]]
  rule "adverb       [[quietly] [bravely] [slowly] [secretly] [happily]]
end

; EXPAND turns a part into a list of words. If the part has a rule, it
; picks one choice and expands every piece of it. If not, the part is
; a word already and we just hand it back. This is the base case.
to expand :part
  localmake "choices gprop "grammar :part
  if emptyp :choices [output (list :part)]
  output map.se [expand ?] pick :choices
end

; The sentence comes out as a list of words with a "." at the end. To
; print it nicely we glue the full stop onto the last word.
to tidy :words
  output lput (word last butlast :words last :words) butlast butlast :words
end

; Capitalize the first letter of the first word.
to capital :words
  localmake "w first :words
  output fput (word uppercase first :w butfirst :w) butfirst :words
end

grammar
print [Ten silly sentences:]
print []
repeat 10 [
  print (sentence (word repcount ".) capital tidy expand "sentence)
]
